.TH FLOWVERSIONS "1" "November 2026" "flowversions" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
flowversions \- list the OPM Flow versions found, and which one a name selects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flowversions
[\fBlist\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B flowversions which
[\fIversion\fR]
.br
.B flowversions show
[\fIversion\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flowversions
finds the Flow versions on this machine and in the configuration, and shows
what each one is and which one a name selects.
.BR runflow (1)
finds and selects versions in exactly the same way, so
\fBflowversions which\fR \fIversion\fR names the Flow that
\fBrunflow \-v\fR \fIversion\fR runs.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBlist\fR (the default)
One line per version found, in the order found (see HOW VERSIONS ARE
FOUND), after a header line:
.RS
.PP
.nf
  NAMES  OPM VERSION  BUILD    FLOW
* dev    2027.04\-pre  Release  /home/user/opm/build/bin/Release/flow
  flow   2026.04      \-        /usr/bin/flow
.fi
.TP
.B *
marks the default version, used without \fB\-v\fR.
.TP
.B NAMES
the names from the configuration, separated by commas; without one, the
names of the executable, e.g. \fBflowrc47\fR.
.TP
.B OPM VERSION
.TQ
.B BUILD
the OPM version and build type (e.g. \fBRelease\fR, \fBDebug\fR), as the
version says itself (see WHAT A VERSION IS); \fB\-\fR where it does not say.
.TP
.B FLOW
the executable, with symbolic links resolved.
.RE
.TP
.BR which " [" \fIversion\fR ]
Prints the executable that \fIversion\fR selects, or without \fIversion\fR
the default version's: one line, for scripts, e.g.
\fB"$(flowversions which dev)" \-\-help\fR.
.TP
.BR show " [" \fIversion\fR ]
Everything known about one version (without \fIversion\fR, the default):
.RS
.TP
.B flow
the executable, with symbolic links resolved;
.TP
.B names
.TQ
.B executable names
the names it can be selected by (see SELECTING A VERSION);
.TP
.B OPM version
.TQ
.B git hash
.TQ
.B build time
.TQ
.B build type
.TQ
.B MPI
what it says about itself (see WHAT A VERSION IS); the git hash ends with
\fB*\fR when it was built from changed sources; MPI is the library it was built
with, or \fBnone\fR;
.TP
.B pythonpath
its \fBpythonpath\fR, or the one \fBsearch.pythonpath\fR gives it, put
before \fBPYTHONPATH\fR when it runs;
.TP
.B launchinfo
the
.BR opmlaunchinfo (1)
that reads its decks for runflow: the configured one, else one in the same
directory as \fBflow\fR; or none, then runflow's own parser reads them;
.TP
.B launcher
the MPI launcher its runs here (\fBrunflow \-i\fR) would use, how it was
found (as configured, the version's \fBlauncher\fR, next to \fBflow\fR, or in
\fBPATH\fR) and
whether it belongs to Flow's MPI (see
.BR runflow (1),
RUNNING);
.TP
.B default
whether it is the default version;
.TP
.B found by
each way it was found: \fBconfig\fR \fIfile\fR, \fBsearch\fR
\fIpattern\fR, \fBPATH\fR, or \fBgiven path\fR.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
For \fBlist\fR and \fBshow\fR: print JSON for other programs (see JSON OUTPUT).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage.
.SH SELECTING A VERSION
\fIversion\fR, here and in \fBrunflow \-v\fR, is tried as, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
a path: a value containing \fB/\fR, or the name of an existing file, e.g.
\fB~/opm/build/bin/flow\fR or \fB./flow\fR;
.IP 2. 4
a name from the configuration, e.g. \fBdev\fR;
.IP 3. 4
an executable's file name, e.g. \fBflowrc47\fR;
.IP 4. 4
an OPM version, e.g. \fB2026.04\fR.
.PP
The first rule that matches decides, so a configured name wins over an
executable that happens to have that name. If one rule matches several
versions, nothing is chosen: the error lists them, and giving one a name in
the configuration tells them apart.
.PP
The default version is \fBdefault_version\fR of the configuration (itself
selected by these rules); without it, the first \fBflow\fR in \fBPATH\fR.
.SH HOW VERSIONS ARE FOUND
In this order:
.IP 1. 4
the named \fBversions\fR of the configuration;
.IP 2. 4
every executable that each pattern of \fBsearch.flow\fR matches, in name
order, e.g. \fB/usr/lib64/openmpi/bin/flow*\fR;
.IP 3. 4
\fBflow\fR in each directory of \fBPATH\fR.
.PP
An executable found more than once, also through a symbolic link, is one
version, with every name and path it was found as. A configured version
whose \fBflow\fR is not an executable, a pattern that matches directories
instead of executables, a directory that cannot be read, or an
executable that disappears while it is looked at, is reported as a warning
and skipped; the others are still found.
.SH WHAT A VERSION IS
Each version is asked about itself, only when it is needed (to list it, or to
run it) and at most once:
.IP 1. 4
\fBflow \-\-build\-info\fR, in Flow versions that have it: the OPM version, git
hash, build time, build type and MPI library;
.IP 2. 4
otherwise \fBflow \-\-version\fR: the OPM version only.
.PP
Both answer without starting MPI, within a fraction of a second. A version
that answers neither is listed without an OPM version.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
\fBlist \-\-json\fR prints \fB{"versions": [\fR...\fB]}\fR, a list of versions
in the order found; \fBshow \-\-json\fR prints one version. A version is an
object with these members; those that are not known are left out:
.TP
\fBflow\fR (string)
the executable, with symbolic links resolved;
.TP
\fBnames\fR, \fBexecutable_names\fR (lists of strings)
.TP
\fBopm_version\fR, \fBgit_hash\fR, \fBbuild_time\fR, \fBbuild_type\fR, \fBmpi\fR (strings)
.TP
\fBpythonpath\fR, \fBlaunchinfo\fR (strings)
.TP
\fBdefault\fR (true or false)
.TP
\fBfound_by\fR (list of strings)
.TP
\fBlauncher\fR (object; \fBshow\fR only)
\fBprogram\fR (the launcher's path) and \fBfound\fR (how), if one was found,
and \fBcheck\fR (the result of the check against Flow's MPI, or why there is
none).
.PP
Members may be added in later versions; programs reading this output should
ignore those they do not know.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fBflowversions\fR reads the same \fBrunflow.json\fR files as
.BR runflow (1):
the installation's, \fB~/.runflow.json\fR, \fBrunflow.json\fR in the current
directory and \fB$RUNFLOW_CONFIG\fR, each overriding the one before. It uses
the version settings and the \fBlocal\fR launcher:
.TP
\fBdefault_version\fR (string)
the default version;
.TP
\fBsearch.flow\fR (list of patterns)
executables that are versions, e.g. \fB["/project/res/bin/flow*"]\fR;
.TP
\fBsearch.pythonpath\fR (list of patterns)
the \fBPYTHONPATH\fR of versions without their own: \fB{name}\fR is the
version's file name, and wildcards match existing directories;
.TP
\fBversions\fR (object of named versions)
each with \fBflow\fR (required), and optionally \fBlaunchinfo\fR,
\fBlauncher\fR and \fBpythonpath\fR (a list);
.TP
\fBlocal.launcher\fR, \fBlocal.launcher_options\fR
the MPI launcher of runs here, shown by \fBshow\fR.
.PP
In lists, \fB"@inherit"\fR stands for the earlier files' list.
.PP
.BR runflow (1),
CONFIGURATION, describes every setting, the files and how they combine, with
examples.
.SH EXAMPLES
Which versions are there, and which is the default:
.PP
.RS
.nf
flowversions
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything about the version \fBrunflow \-v 2026.04\fR would run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
flowversions show 2026.04
.fi
.RE
.PP
The OPM versions of all versions, in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
flowversions list \-\-json | python3 \-c \(aqimport json, sys
for v in json.load(sys.stdin)["versions"]:
    print(v.get("opm_version", "?"), v["flow"])\(aq
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error, after the output.
.TP
\fBFlow version not found:\fR \fIversion\fR
No rule of SELECTING A VERSION matches: \fBflowversions list\fR shows what is
there.
.TP
\fBFlow version\fR \fIversion\fR \fBis ambiguous\fR
One rule matches several versions, listed after the message: select one by
its path, or name it in the configuration.
.TP
\fBwarning:\fR ...
A problem that did not stop the search, e.g. a configured version that is not
an executable, a search pattern that matches directories, a directory that
cannot be read, or a setting of a
newer runflow.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a version is not found or is ambiguous; 2 for wrong
arguments, or a configuration file that cannot be read (not valid JSON, a
setting of the wrong type, or \fB$RUNFLOW_CONFIG\fR naming a missing file).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR runflow (1),
.BR opmlaunchinfo (1)
